What is the grant type often used for server-server interaction? Client Credentials

English Short Note
OAuth 2.0 Grant Types Summary
Authorization Code Grant: The most secure flow, heavily used for server-side applications (like Node.js, Spring Boot). The client receives a
temporary authorization code, which the backend server exchanges securely for an access token along with a client secret. Protects the token
from exposure to the user's browser.
Implicit Grant: Designed for Single Page Applications (SPAs) and mobile apps that cannot securely store a client secret. Returns the access
token directly in the URL fragment. Inherently less secure due to the token being exposed to the user agent.
Resource Owner Password Credentials Grant: The user provides their raw username and password directly to the client, which exchanges
them for a token. Strictly reserved for highly trusted, first-party applications.
Client Credentials Grant: Exclusively used for machine-to-machine (server-to-server) communication where no user is involved. The backend
authenticates itself using its client ID and client secret to obtain a token.

